Register a dynamic-slice offset as an encoder temporary only when its
storage was newly allocated.

When the start indices are donatable, `compute_dynamic_offset` shares
their buffer with the offset output. Registering that donated alias as an
encoder temporary makes `CommandEncoder::end_encoding()` drop the shared
pointer from the input and output dependency sets before producer fences
are resolved, so a dynamic slice can read a stale start across a
command-buffer boundary. This corrupts `lax.scan` stacked-output history
when the read- and write-side start chains are CSE-shared.

Upstreamed as ml-explore/mlx#4099 (merged 2026-08-11); drop this patch at
the next MLX bump past that commit.

diff --git a/mlx/backend/metal/slicing.cpp b/mlx/backend/metal/slicing.cpp
--- a/mlx/backend/metal/slicing.cpp
+++ b/mlx/backend/metal/slicing.cpp
@@ -57,9 +57,9 @@ array compute_dynamic_offset(
   if (donate) {
     offset.copy_shared_buffer(indices);
   } else {
     offset.set_data(allocator::malloc(offset.itemsize()));
+    compute_encoder.add_temporary(offset);
   }
-  compute_encoder.add_temporary(offset);
 
   auto dtype = indices.dtype();
   std::string lib_name = "compute_dynamic_offset_" + type_to_name(dtype);
